<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tanagra figurine</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Tanagra figurines</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tanagra figurines are Greek terracotta statuettes, mostly of elegantly dressed women, made from the later 4th century BCE and named after Tanagra in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boeotia">Boeotia</a>, where many were found in tombs in the 1870s. Cast in moulds and painted, they show ordinary people rather than gods. Their discovery made them highly fashionable among 19th-century collectors and led to many forgeries.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tanagra-figurine.jpg" alt="Tanagra figurine" width="320" height="752" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Workshop of the Lady in Blue · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lady_in_blue_Louvre_MNB907.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
